# Keep binary files byte-identical through a clone.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows machines this repo is developed on, so
# git rewrites line endings on checkout for anything it decides is text. It
# decides by looking for a NUL byte in the first 8 KiB, and a PDF's header and
# object table are plain ASCII — so `git add` of the report printed:
#
#     warning: ... LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it
#
# The committed blob is correct; a fresh clone on Windows would not be. Every
# lone 0x0A inside the compressed streams would become 0x0D 0x0A, the byte
# offsets in the xref table would no longer point at the objects, and the file
# would open as a damaged PDF. The same trap applies to any asset git happens
# to sniff as text.
#
# Marking these `binary` (which is shorthand for -text -diff) turns the
# conversion off and stops git trying to diff them as lines.

*.pdf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.mp4  binary
*.mov  binary
*.webm binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
*.db   binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
